Parent: 

The PAIRRANKER module is a novel approach for parent ranking in natural language processing. It addresses the problem of selecting the best candidate from a pool of candidates by considering all pairwise comparisons among them, rather than comparing each candidate with the input independently as done in pointwise methods. The Parent model and child models are trained to optimize towards multiple Q-functions simultaneously, which can be seen as a multi-task classification task. 

The PAIRRANKER module is designed for parent ranking tasks where there are more than two candidates per input. It differs from the pointwise methods in that it encodes both the input and the pair of candidates at the same time, enabling the model to capture the difference between them in the context of the input. 

The architecture of PAIRRANKER is as follows:
Parent: 

1. The Parent module takes an input x and a pair of candidates yi and yj, which are represented by their embedd vice scores si(i, j) and sj(i, j), respectively. These values represent the confidence that candidate i (yi) is better than candidate j (yj). 

The parent model and child models are trained to optimize multiple Q-functions simultaneously, treating it as a multi-task classification problem: LQ = -log(si(i,j)) - zjlog(sj(i,j)), where the sigmoid function is denoted by \u03c. 

The training process involves randomly selecting some combinations from the candidate pool Y2 instead of all pairs in order to ensure learning efficiency. The model learns to be consistent with itself despite the position embeddings of the language model, so we shuffle the order of candidates within each training pair during training. 

During inference, we obtain scores sij for each pair of candidates (yi and yj) from the candidate pool Y2. After N(N-1)/2 comparisons, we select the best candidate based on three aggregation functions: MaxLogits, which calculates the confidence that yi is superior to all other candidates; and MaxWins, which counts the number of victories in comparisons with other candidates. 

However, these two methods necessitate O(N^2) iterations for Ncandidates, which can be computationally expensive. Thus, we propose a more efficient aggregation method, performing a single bubble sort run with pairwise comparisons to select the best candidate. We first shuffle the order of candidates within each training pair so that the model learns to be consistent with itself. 

Regardless of the aggregation method, we can rank all candidates in Y. Our experiments reveal that MaxLogits yields the best performance, so we use it as the default aggregator for P AIRRANKER .
